# Patch X.Y — Rollback procedure

## Code rollback

```bash
# 1. Find the merge commit for patch-X.Y on main
git log --merges --oneline | grep "patch-X.Y"
# → <sha> Merge branch 'patch-X.Y'

# 2. Revert it on main
git revert -m 1 <sha>
```

This restores the source tree to the pre-patch state. Any patches that
depend on this one (`depends_on` chain) must be rolled back FIRST.

## Data rollback

Most migrations in this patch are additive (new columns, new tables) and
do not need explicit rollback. The list below documents whether each
migration is reversible and, if so, how:

| Migration                  | Reversible? | Rollback SQL                                |
|----------------------------|-------------|---------------------------------------------|
| `001_*.sql`                | Yes / No    | <SQL or "leave columns; harmless">          |
| `002_*.sql`                | Yes / No    | <SQL>                                       |

**Hard rule:** if a migration drops a column or rewrites historical data
(`UPDATE` that replaces values), capture a `.sql.bak` of the affected
rows BEFORE running it. The framework does not do this for you.

## After rollback — verification

- [ ] `python -m py_compile` on each touched file
- [ ] Pipeline runs without erroring on the removed feature
- [ ] Any monitoring that was added is gracefully absent (not crashing)
- [ ] Edit `backend/patches/X.Y/manifest.json`: set `applied_at` to `""`
- [ ] Commit `chore(patch-X.Y): rolled back on YYYY-MM-DD`
